# Landing anti-slop

Never ship the centroid SaaS look. Prefer specificity over “clean template.”

## Forbidden on the landing

- Pill / chip badges above the hero (`rounded-full border … text-xs`, status dots, slogan chips)
- Uppercase tracked “eyebrow” labels (`text-xs uppercase tracking-wider`) as section intros, demo labels (“Try it”), or card headers
- Identical feature cards (`rounded-2xl border bg-card` + icon tile + title + two lines), especially in equal grids
- Soft purple / indigo / blue brand gradients and decorative radial glow blobs as the main atmosphere
- Grandiose empty slogans (“the way it should have always been,” “reimagine,” “unlock your potential”)
- Hero stacks with more than one primary CTA group plus multiple ghost links competing
- Long muted “supporting” paragraphs that restate the headline
- Hero copy that leads with What (features, integrations, “connect X and Y”) instead of Why

## Prefer instead

- Golden Circle (Simon Sinek): lead with **Why**, then **How**, then **What**. Never open the hero with features or integrations
- First screen (`min-h-dvh`): brand + Why headline + scroll cue only. Rams / Ive restraint. No waitlist form, no supporting blurb in the hero body
- Floating auth CTA top-right always (Sign in / Sign up, or Go to app). Header logo + blur + border reveal only after scroll
- Brand first in the hero stage, then a Why headline (belief / purpose). No supporting paragraph under it
- Quiet scroll indicator (sentence case “Scroll”, subtle motion, fade after user scrolls). Never uppercase eyebrows
- How section: short step titles only (the process), not product pitches
- What surfaces later as demos, boards, and CTAs (GitHub, LinkedIn, tailor, track), not as the first-viewport pitch
- Flat sections, numbered lists, or asymmetric layouts instead of card grids
- Serif display for headings (existing landing pattern), concrete verbs, section padding that varies
- Open source / GitHub as a real strip with repo CTAs, not marketing copy
- Shared `max-w-6xl` page width across landing sections

## Copy test

If the first viewport could belong to another AI career product after removing the logo, rewrite it.
If the hero names tools or features (GitHub, LinkedIn, tailor a CV), you are in What. Move that down and rewrite as Why.
If you need a second sentence under a headline to explain the product, put How/What into later sections, not under the hero.
